{"language": "en", "duration": 87.44, "text": "You find $100 in a jacket, a little smile, and the day goes on. You lose $100, the whole day is ruined, yet it's exactly the same amount. This reflex has a name, loss aversion. To your brain, losing hurts more than winning feels good. On average, a loss weighs about twice as much as an equal gain. Researchers Daniel Canemann and Amos Tversky showed it with a simple coin flip. Heads, you win $100, tails, you lose $100. Most people say no. To get them to accept, you have to promise about $200 in winnings. And it gets expensive. We hold on to a stock that's collapsing, hoping to get back to our purchase price rather than accept the loss. And we sell the winners too early for fear of losing the gain. Retailers know it. Only two left in stock offer ends tonight. You're not buying to gain something. You're buying so you don't lose it. So how do you take back control? First, wait 24 hours before any purchase made under pressure. Then ask yourself, if I didn't already own it, would I buy it today? Finally, set your rules in advance when you're calm. Your budget and the price at which you sell. Losing hurts. That's human. But a decision made to escape the pain isn't always the best one. And you? Which loss has already pushed you into a bad decision?", "words": [{"word": "You", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.46}, {"word": "find", "start": 0.46, "end": 0.86}, {"word": "a", "start": 0.86, "end": 1.13}, {"word": "hundred", "start": 1.13, "end": 1.39}, {"word": "dollars", "start": 1.39, "end": 1.66}, {"word": "in", "start": 1.66, "end": 2.14}, {"word": "a", "start": 2.14, "end": 2.24}, {"word": "jacket:", "start": 2.24, "end": 2.52}, {"word": "a", "start": 2.92, "end": 3.04}, {"word": "little", "start": 3.04, "end": 3.2}, {"word": "smile,", "start": 3.2, "end": 3.58}, {"word": "and", "start": 4.14, "end": 4.14}, {"word": "the", "start": 4.14, "end": 4.28}, {"word": "day", "start": 4.28, "end": 4.5}, {"word": "goes", "start": 4.5, "end": 4.72